  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nMidday surface analysis depicted a well-defined cold front, that\nhad already cleared much of SW KS. Northeast winds behind the\ncold front were above all guidance, per usual, gusting near\n30 mph, but are expected to weaken by late afternoon as \npressure gradients begin to weaken. 12z ARW/NAM have caught on \nto this faster cold frontal timing, with any convergence on the \nboundary expected to be near the Oklahoma border by peak heating\nlate this afternoon. Indeed, most if not all convection may end\nup in Oklahoma. Upstream air mass across the central plains is \nquite cool and dry for September, and it appears models are \nstruggling to capture the cold front's advance. Confined pops \nfurther to the far southeast zones this evening. A small window \nof opportunity will exist for a marginally severe storm across \nthe southeast counties, either along the surface convergence \nzone, or elevated north of the front. High end/significant \nsevere weather is unlikely. Temperatures this afternoon will \nstruggle to reach NBM guidance in the cooler northeast winds. \n\nIncreasing midlevel clouds are expected tonight north of the\nstalling frontal boundary, but dry weather will prevail with\nSunday sunrise temperatures in the 60s. \n\nSome temperature uncertainty is apparent in the forecast for\nSunday, with models disagreeing on where the nearly stationary\nfrontal boundary will end up through the afternoon. NAM\nsolutions are much cooler, with the front remaining near the\nOklahoma border through Sunday. Given afternoon sunshine, and\n850 mb temperatures still near 20C, feel MET guidance of 70s\nnorth and 80s south is too cold, but NBM is probably too hot.\nGlobal models are more enthusiastic returning the front to near\nUS-50 by late afternoon, so opted for 80s northeast counties\nranging to near 100 near Oklahoma. A strong capping inversion\n(700 mb temps near 16C) is expected to prevent convective\ninitiation on the boundary Sunday afternoon. Still, an isolated\nstorm or two is possible Sunday evening as the low level jet\ncranks up and starts shoving the boundary northward, and kept\nNBM's minimal pops for this possibility. \n\nMonday will be hottest day of this forecast period, with near\nrecord highs near 100 expected. Pressure gradients will support\nstrong southwest winds Monday, averaging 25-35 mph, gusting to\nnear 45 mph. With this downslope compression acting on a hot\nwell mixed boundary layer, temperatures will soar to record\nlevels Monday, and added 1-2 degrees to NBM guidance. \n\nStrong shortwave trough crossing the northern plains Monday\nnight will send the next cold front into SW KS Tuesday.\nScattered thunderstorms are expected to develop along the\nfront Tuesday afternoon, with strong to marginally severe wind\nand/or hail possible. A large temperature range is correctly\nforecast by NBM, ranging from 75 north to near 100 in Barber\ncounty. NBM pops are into the definite/categorical range (near\n80%) Tuesday night, with various signals of MCS development\nacross at least portions of Kansas. Will need to monitor \nTuesday night for heavy rain/hydrology and strong storm\nconcerns. Additional rain and thunderstorms are likely Wednesday\nnight, as SWly midlevel flow is maintained. With clouds, rain\nchances, and the midlevel summer ridge removed well east of \nSW KS, afternoon temperatures should be restricted to the 80s\nfor much of next week.",
